A||||12||MooCow||Winter Driving?||||5||
Z||000000||MooCow||11-08-2000||07:39 PM||||I have a couple of questions for ya.<BR>How hard is winter driving in the Fiero?<BR>I am asking because I am a teenager that can't wait till next year to get my car done so I was thinking of selling my Ranger which would give me like $3,300 to play with.  That money would get me a 3.4 with a 4-speed with a 4:10 gear ratio.  Byt then I would have to drive it in the winter.  The thing is the lady I bought this from drove this car year round anyways.  I do live in Michigain and winter's are not to bad.  I consider myself a good driver in the winter so what do you think.  Should I do it or not?<P>                          Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||11-08-2000||11:07 PM||||Well, since you asked...<P>Fieros aren't bad in the winter.  But they're not ideal; they can be less than forgiving.<P>If I'm not mistaken, you currently own a dead Fiero, and a living Ford Ranger - ?  You are a good winter driver.  You are thinking you can get a 3.4 swapped in and ready to go soon.  The Fiero would be approximately 17.378 times more fun than the Ranger.<P>But I'd stick with the Ranger for now, for several reasons.  One, even though you are a good winter driver, a lot of people out there are not.  The Ranger is a vehicle which people will actually <B>see</B> -  a very important safety feature.<P>The Ranger is probably newer and thus more reliable.  Also, engine swaps take more time and effort and money than you think (or so I've read here).  You're young - you've plenty of time to do the engine swap thing next summer.<P>For now, you might think about getting the money for the swap some other way, rather than selling off a perfectly good running vehicle.<P>Now, if somebody runs into your Ranger this the winter, and you come out in one piece, oh well, you'll get over it.  If someone hits your Fiero, it'd be downright tragic  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  That's why people have "winter" cars.  JMHO - and plenty of it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.          ||||reg||4
Z||000002||Standard||11-09-2000||03:24 AM||||but what if your winter car is another Fiero? hehe.  I should really concentrate on getting the '84 running now that we got some freakin' snow.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  and the heater in the '84 actually works, too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||11-10-2000||04:08 AM||||Just found this over at LS1.com..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>10. Two words: LIABILITY INSURANCE<BR>9. Car washes a thing of the past<BR>8. Automatic theft deterrent system. Rest assured that when you leave it to go into the mall,that POS will still be there when you come back.<BR>7. Your oil changes itself every 300 miles. <BR>6. Wax? Your kidding right?<BR>5. Be the prick you always wanted to be and park it in the tightest parking spot you can find without fear of door dings or keys!<BR>4. Increased room for parking. When you pull into Dayton's parking lot you can feel confident that the adjacent spots next to you will be vacated,ASAP.<BR>3. Easy of getting into the passing lane. When that Volvo sees your POS(probably uninsured) Sh!tbox signaling; THEY WILL MOVE THE HELL OVER!!<BR>2. Add value to your car by adding more gasoline to the tank.<BR>1. And the #1 reason to drive a winter beater is: Super tech oil, 74 cents a quart at Wal Mart!!!!!!!!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||Philphine||11-10-2000||11:43 AM||||whoever wrote that list must have seen my s-10 blazer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||11-10-2000||11:58 PM||||I wouldn't suggest driving a winter Fiero if it's nice.  Mine was out of gas, so my brother and I went to the station and got a couple gallons.  We filled it up at home, and I was moving it into the other garage to cover it for storage.  I was kinda screwing around, and I took a corner, going from one driveway to another, a little too fast.  The corner is about a 45* angle, so it's kinda bad.  I took it at about 25.  I must have undercalculated the corner, as the front left tire went off the side of the driveway.  We have some snow up here in Minnesota, so the grass was a little wet.  The tire went through the snow, and it came back on the driveway while I was turning.  The driveway was a little wet also, so all of a sudden, the back end slid to the right.  I tried to countersteer, but the rear came back too fast, so it was now sliding to the left.  I knew I was going in the trees on the side of the driveway, and since I was sliding in the grass, I knew I would hit one of the many trees.  I used a driving method I learned from CHiPs on TNT; I punched the gas all the way to the floor.  This stopped me from sliding a little bit, but I still went into the "woods".  I ended up coming about a foot from three trees.  They for sure would have totaled the left side.  I still received moderate scratches, but I used GS27, and it took them ALL out.  That's some good stuff.  I thought I was going to need another repaint.  Anyway, the moral of this long story is that Fieros don't handle the best in slippery conditions.  I know I'm not too experienced with this type of thing, but I did a 180 on a frickin half an inch of snow.  If your Fiero's nice, I would park it and keep the Ranger. <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000006||WisconsinGT||11-11-2000||01:51 AM||||Quote of the year:<BR>"I used a driving method I learned from CHiPs on TNT"<P>Damn! I love this forum.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Standard||11-11-2000||01:59 AM||||I drove my '84 all last winter, and it wasn't bad at all.  Never got stuck, and I never got into a bad situation.  Just take some time in an empty parking lot, or a neighborhood that has no traffic and learn how it reacts.  And know your limits, too!  Don't try taking any off-ramps at the speeds you take them during the summer, and ease into the throttle at the end.||||reg||1
Z||000008||onfire||11-11-2000||03:17 AM||||I took my 85 gt up some logging roads. With a few inches of snow. No problems what so ever. Since most of the weight of the Fiero is in the rear it has very good traction to get the car moving. Its stopping that people have trouble with. So give yourself twice the distance between you and other cars that you normally would. And definately practice in an empty parking lot.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/files/SNOW.JPG"> <BR>It got really deep about two miles later.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by onfire (edited 11-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||JetroGT||11-11-2000||07:14 AM||||Winter driving? Not unless you really have to. Black Ice on the interstate and concrete dividing walls can really hurt your fiero, and your neck if you get wiplash as bad as I did. <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smash2.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smash1.jpg"> <P>||||reg||11
Z||000010||CraZyRabBit||11-11-2000||11:05 AM||||Ken, depends on the condition of your Fiero. Do they use salt where you live? If it's been driven every winter with road salt and depending what model you own it may not be worth while to give up your Ranger and throw 3 grand at your Fiero. Also, if it's a GT in very good condition I wouldn't let it see snow. If it's a POS, SELL IT!! Take your 3 grand and buy a really nice GT instead. Anyway, no matter what you drive, I think it's best to keep both vehicles. I love Fieros but I wouldn't have it as my only everyday car. Fiero's are best kept as a hobby car, my .02 cents.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||fropuff||11-11-2000||12:08 PM||||I know some of you are out there are thinking I'm a retard for taking that corner so fast, but I guess I learned from my mistake.  I will NEVER do that again.  I never planned on taking my Fiero out in the winter; I was just putting it in the back garage.  I know, I was screwing around, and I deserved what happened, but sometimes that is more beneficial to you.  I get my license in February, and I'm going to drive a 1980 Oldsmobile Cutlass, rear wheel drive.  I know how this car performs in the snow and ice, as I've had it since I was thirteen.  I am a pretty good driver in the snow and stuff, but I just wasn't used to the Fiero.  That's the key.  You should go out, like Standard said, and do all the stupid stuff that people do in the winter.  This will give you a better idea on how to handle it when you are put in an emergency situation.  Or, you could watch an episode of CHiPs on TNT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Sometimes watching TV is beneficial as well.  If I would have just let my car do what it wanted, I would be a pissed little boy today.  By my little knowledge of what to do, I recevered somewhat and saved my Fiero from a larger accident.  The Fiero can be a good winter vehicle, with its design and everything, but you have to know how to drive it.  If I would have known how to drive mine, I wouldn't be getting the scratches out today.  Have fun, be safe, watch CHiPs.    ||||reg||1
Z||000012||wkayl||11-12-2000||11:47 AM||||Yesterday (Sat) i drove my Fiero here in Colorado to go pick up my new 4x4 pickup.  The Fiero is all I had to drive because last Monday I decided to park my 90 chev 4x4 in an irrigation ditch.  Anyway, the Fiero handled the 4 inches of snow like crap.  The most problem I had was in towns where the corners had snow built up and sloppy.  We made it, but left it at the dealer to pick up when the roads are clear.  So MrFiero, if you see a red 86 GT at Purifoy Chev, it's not for sale.  I'll post a picture of my former and new trucks in the o/t section after I get the new one washed up.  ||||reg||1
